WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.925
Production improvement requires visibility into what the loop actually did.

2
00:00:04.925 --> 00:00:15.268
A final output alone does not explain why the loop succeeded, why it failed, or why it spent more than expected.

3
00:00:15.268 --> 00:00:21.671
Without traces, engineers cannot diagnose inefficient context selection, unsafe proposals, unnecessary tool calls,

4
00:00:21.671 --> 00:00:26.596
incorrect completion decisions, or policy rules that block useful work.

5
00:00:26.596 --> 00:00:31.029
A trace is a structured record of a run.

6
00:00:31.029 --> 00:00:37.431
It may include task metadata, model and configuration version, context references, proposed actions,

7
00:00:37.431 --> 00:00:43.834
policy decisions, tool calls, tool results, state transitions, evaluation outcomes, human approvals, timing,

8
00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:47.282
token and cost data, and terminal status.

9
00:00:47.282 --> 00:00:56.639
The trace should avoid storing secrets or hidden reasoning, but it should preserve enough operational evidence to investigate behavior.

10
00:00:56.639 --> 00:01:04.027
Good traces let teams debug the harness rather than guessing from the final answer alone.

11
00:01:04.027 --> 00:01:11.415
Useful metrics include task success rate, valid completion rate, false completion rate, human escalation rate,

12
00:01:11.415 --> 00:01:16.833
policy-block rate, average iterations, tool-error rate, retry rate, no-progress termination rate,

13
00:01:16.833 --> 00:01:23.728
cost per successful task, latency, frequency of human correction, and regression rate after changes.

14
00:01:23.728 --> 00:01:26.190
Metrics must be interpreted together.

15
00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.638
Lower cost can hide more false completions.

16
00:01:29.638 --> 00:01:34.563
Higher success can reflect an evaluator that became too permissive.

17
00:01:34.563 --> 00:01:37.518
Representative traces should become evaluation datasets.

18
00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:44.906
Convert successes, failures, edge cases, and security incidents into repeatable tests before changing prompts, tools,

19
00:01:44.906 --> 00:01:47.861
models, context policy, or control logic.

20
00:01:47.861 --> 00:01:55.249
Then change one relevant harness component, rerun the evaluation set, and compare quality, safety, cost,

21
00:01:55.249 --> 00:01:58.204
and latency against a versioned baseline.

22
00:01:58.204 --> 00:02:03.129
Changing several components at once makes causal analysis much harder.

23
00:02:03.129 --> 00:02:07.562
Versioned evaluations turn anecdotes into a repeatable improvement process.

24
00:02:07.562 --> 00:02:17.905
In the coding loop, traces may reveal that the system rereads too many files before every test or repeatedly asks for

25
00:02:17.905 --> 00:02:21.352
broad repository context after a small failure.

26
00:02:21.352 --> 00:02:28.740
The fix may be a narrower file-search tool, better context policy, or improved progress evaluation,

27
00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:31.203
not simply a longer instruction.

28
00:02:31.203 --> 00:02:33.173
Loop engineering is empirical.

29
00:02:33.173 --> 00:02:40.561
Reliability improves when changes respond to observed failure patterns and are measured against stable criteria.
